      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could we leave drop rewrites unclassified? The input and output slow paths set `ts_rw_bm` before detecting an unavailable backend, unavailable SNAT address, or exhausted ports. Those cached failed flows therefore reach this helper and increment `/cnat/flows/nat`, although the documentation says failed flows contribute only to `total`. The direct VIP/SNAT paths already guard against `*_NEXT_DROP`. Please also add negative coverage asserting the new error counter increments while all class gauges remain zero.",
      "revId": "bd5cbd978767eca88a7facac93736da88247ecba",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5ac11975_f9b0e1bd",
        "filename": "src/plugins/cnat/cnat_types.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 295,
      "author": {
        "id": 361
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-17T09:09:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could we use the usual `vlib_simple_counter_main_t` infrastructure instead of global atomic counters here? Cross-thread expiration does not require decrementing the creator\u0027s slot: maintain monotonic per-thread `created`/`classified` and `expired` counters on whichever thread performs each operation, then compute the active gauge as `sum(created) - sum(expired)` in the collector. This avoids contention on global cache lines and preserves the standard per-thread lifecycle counters. The per-flow CAS can remain to ensure classification happens once; the collector should clamp transient scrape skew at zero.",
      "revId": "bd5cbd978767eca88a7facac93736da88247ecba",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
    }
